+/// GPU time of a submission past which the next ones go one title list per
+/// host submission. The host resets a device whose job runs for about 2 s
+/// (amdgpu's default `lockup_timeout`); a world frame of a few dozen lists
+/// in one submission passes that while pipelines and caches are cold, one
+/// list at a time does not.
+const SPLIT_AFTER: std::time::Duration = std::time::Duration::from_millis(400);
+
+/// Submissions still to go one list at a time after a slow one (see
+/// [`SPLIT_AFTER`]). One list at a time each runs briefly, so their times
+/// cannot say whether the whole would be slow again: a slow submission keeps
+/// splitting on for [`SPLIT_FOR`] more (about ten world frames), and every
+/// slow one starts the count again. Toggling off after the first quick list
+/// sent the next frame whole again (r12: 3.4 s, device removed). With
+/// asynchronous submissions a slow one is also only seen once it finished,
+/// a few submissions late.
+static SPLIT_LEFT: std::sync::atomic::AtomicU32 = std::sync::atomic::AtomicU32::new(0);
